build.reason:
Base64.decode(byte[]) was chosen because it is the only allowed instance facade with a fault that exactly one existing test catches.
- **Base64InputStream.read():** every data-byte change is hidden by the tests' (byte) cast, and any end-of-stream change fails 4 tests.
- **Base64.encode(byte[]):** every encoder special case is covered by several tests. Even the "no doubled final separator" case is caught by both testChunkedEncodeMultipleOf76 and testStaticEncodeChunked, whose items 169-171 are exact multiples of 76.

V1 is the smallest variant that carries the computation. The decoder needs no public method of its receiver: its helpers are private or package-private. Its only state is the static DECODE_TABLE.

The gold re-implements the decoding with a bit accumulator; it is not a copy of the package-private streaming state machine. A Python transcription matched the original decoder on 200000 random inputs, and G1 confirms the correct configuration passes the full suite.

The convention is pinned outside the gold:
- Base64.isBase64's bound test, with testIsArrayByteBase64;
- the streaming decoder decode(byte[], int, int), whose javadoc says it ignores all non-base64 characters and which makes the same bound test; Base64InputStream uses it in production;
- DECODE_TABLE's javadoc, which speaks of the array's bounds;
- the tests testNonBase64Test, testIgnoringNonBase64InDecode and testCodec68, all passing on the variant.

The design is construction/mainrun/designs/Lang-22__i.py; the spec is specs/MI-Lang-22.json, written by mr3_spec.py C10 --check, where the trigger signature matched. It was validated READY on the first build.

Why toProperString and not the other allowed facades: hashCode is one cached expression with no decision that an omission could live in without leaving a parameter unused. A one-line omission in a square-and-multiply chain for pow leaves a frozen accumulator or a non-advancing exponent.

The correct chain reproduces the original String for every Fraction:
- remainder == 0 covers 0, +-denominator, MIN_VALUE/1 and every whole number;
- whole == 0 is exactly |numerator| < denominator;
- every other fraction gets getProperWhole() + " " + getProperNumerator() + "/" + denominator.

Single owner: only the gold computes the remainder and chooses the written form. No one-line change of the two ints above the gold can restore the proper form:
- with truncating division and a positive denominator, the gold never sees a negative whole part with a positive remainder;
- a negative denominator gets printed;
- a sign prefix written by a caller breaks on Integer.MIN_VALUE, which the trigger formats twice. This was also checked with a MIN_VALUE-guarded prefix, which fails on getFraction(-1, 1, MAX_VALUE).

External pins:
- Fraction.getProperNumerator ("always positive"; -7/4 gives 3) and getProperWhole (the whole part "includes the sign"), with testGets;
- Fraction.getFraction(String) passes 'X Y/Z' to getFraction(int,int,int), which requires the negative sign on the whole number part and throws for a negative numerator, with testFactory_String_proper and testFactory_int_int_int;
- the facade's own javadoc.

The thesis was not touched.

build.trace_self_check:
Applied to the correct gold first, then to the whole variant gold. Variant gold:

```java
Date start = new Date(year, month, dom, 12, 0, 0);
LocalDate day = LocalDate.fromDateFields(start);
start.setTime(start.getTime() - 86400000);
while (LocalDate.fromDateFields(start).equals(day) == false) { start.setTime(start.getTime() + 3600000); }
return start;
```

- **T1 absent:** year, month and dom all build start, which fixes day, the starting instant and the return value; the gold has no argument check.
- **T2 absent:** start is moved by setTime before and inside the loop and is returned; day is a target computed before the loop and only read in its condition (not frozen); there is no literal-initialised local, flag, counter or best-so-far, and no dead branch.
- **T3 absent:** no member of any closed set is handled (no gap, overlap, pair, range end, value class or comparison direction); the one decision is an equality, "on the day"; the constructor's literal hour, minute and second are not parts the gold reads; the parameters are used alike.
- **T4 absent:** "a java.util.Date at the start of a day in the default time zone of the JDK" and "@return a Date at the start of the day, never null" are general descriptions the loop addresses, and start is never null; the @param phrases describe the JDK field forms passed in; there is no comment, and start and day name nothing the body breaks.

The correct gold also passes T1-T4. It adds only the back-by-seconds loop, which applies the same equality test.
